   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the public health law, in relation to reporting of alcohol\noverdose data; and to amend chapter 66 of the laws of 2016, amending the\npublic health law, relating to reporting of opioid overdose data, in relation\nto making such provisions permanent",
    "basePrintNo": "S6571",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6571-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §3309, Pub Health L; amd §2, Chap 66 of 2016",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S6571\n\nSPONSOR: HINCHEY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public health law, in relation to reporting of alco-\nhol overdose data; and to amend chapter 66 of the laws of 2016, amending\nthe public health law, relating to reporting of opioid overdose data, in\nrelation to making such provisions permanent\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThis bill will expand the requirements for reporting of overdose data\nand make the requirement for quarterly reporting permanent.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one amends subdivision 5 of section 3309 of the public health\nlaw.\n\nSection two amends subdivision 6 of section 3309 of the public health\nlaw.\n\nSection three amends paragraphs (c) and (d) of subdivision 3-a of\nsection 3309 of the public health law. Section four amends section 2 of\nchapter 66 of the laws of 2016 to make quarterly reporting permanent\nSection five establishes the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nAs opioid overdose deaths have grown at an alarming rate, legislation\nwas enacted in 2016 to require reporting by the Department of Health on\noverdoses and overdose deaths, including information on age, gender,\nethnicity, and the geographic location of overdoses. This data, broken\ndown by county, is required to be shared on a quarterly basis and in a\ncomprehensive annual report.\n\nHowever, in recent years, there has been an increase in overdoses occur-\nring with drugs other than or in addition to opioids, including stimu-\nlants and benzodiazepines. It is critical that this data also be\nincluded in the quarterly reports so that the State and local govern-\nments can better allocate resources to combat overdose.  Nationwide,\noverdose rates have risen during the COVID-19 pandemic, likely driven by\nlimited access to treatment and recovery programs, interruptions of\ntreatment regimens and social isolation. Quarterly reports by the\nDepartment of Health on overdoses and overdose deaths are key to provid-\ning a robust response in a timely manner and must be made permanent.\n\nThis legislation will ensure quarterly reports include data on overdoses\nrelated to non-opioid drugs and will make such reporting requirement\nPermanent-\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nUndetermined.\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately\n",
